From 7b247eadaba0c5603111db7c0edaf17c4b8d9230 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Max Schettler <max@simplyblock.io>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 13:41:44 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] nfsd: never wait for a layout break with an nfsd thread held
 in COPY

A COPY into a file that still carries another client's pNFS write layout
breaks that layout the same way an ordinary write does, and XFS waits for
it with the calling thread held (xfs_break_leased_layouts, by way of
vfs_copy_file_range's splice or reflink fallback). Commit 3448b03
(pnfs-common: nfsd never waits for a layout break with a thread held) fixed
this for nfsd_setattr, nfsd4_vfs_fallocate, and nfsd_vfs_write, three call
sites that all extend or remap a file the same way, but never reached
nfsd_copy_file_range, which sits right above nfsd4_vfs_fallocate in the
same file and has the identical hazard.

For a synchronous COPY this merely reproduces the bug 3448b03 fixed, bounded
by nfsd's own layout-recall timeout. For an asynchronous one it does not
reproduce it: the copy runs in its own kthread (nfsd4_do_async_copy, not an
nfsd service thread), so the wait never starves the thread pool the way
3448b03 described, but the kthread itself blocks uninterruptibly and never
returns, so nfsd4_send_cb_offload is never reached and CB_OFFLOAD is never
sent. The client's handle_async_copy then waits for a reply that will not
come, and because the wait is uninterruptible, the thread and its OS thread
are never released.

First seen as an S3 CompleteMultipartUpload that never returned: the join
of two uploaded parts is a copy_file_range the gateway's posix backend
issues against the destination object's file, which still carried the
write layout the gateway's own preceding writes had taken out on it. Every
pNFS ReadWriteMany consumer is equally exposed: an ordinary cp between two
files on the same pNFS mount reaches the same copy_file_range.

The fix is the same as 3448b03's: start the break without waiting, and
answer the caller with an error it already knows how to turn into a retry
rather than block. nfsd_copy_file_range returns a negative errno on failure
like any other ssize_t-returning VFS helper, and nfsd4_do_copy already maps
a negative return into an NFS4 error when nothing was copied yet
(status = nfserrno(bytes)), for both the synchronous and asynchronous
caller, so no further plumbing is needed: the synchronous RPC answers with
that error directly, and the asynchronous kthread carries it into
nfsd4_send_cb_offload instead of hanging before ever reaching it.
---
 fs/nfsd/vfs.c | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/fs/nfsd/vfs.c b/fs/nfsd/vfs.c
index 46c526f..a78edc0 100644
--- a/fs/nfsd/vfs.c
+++ b/fs/nfsd/vfs.c
@@ -710,6 +710,21 @@ ssize_t nfsd_copy_file_range(struct file *src, u64 src_pos, struct file *dst,
 {
 	ssize_t ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * A copy into this file breaks the pNFS layouts other clients hold on
+	 * it the same way a write does, and the filesystem would wait for
+	 * them with this thread held: an ordinary nfsd thread for a
+	 * synchronous copy, the dedicated copy thread of nfsd4_do_async_copy
+	 * for an asynchronous one. See nfsd_vfs_write: start the break
+	 * without waiting and have the client retry while layouts are still
+	 * out. Unlike a blocked nfsd thread, a blocked copy thread never
+	 * times out on its own, and never reaches nfsd4_send_cb_offload, so
+	 * the client's wait for CB_OFFLOAD does not time out either.
+	 */
+	ret = break_layout(file_inode(dst), false);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
 	/*
 	 * Limit copy to 4MB to prevent indefinitely blocking an nfsd
 	 * thread and client rpc slot.  The choice of 4MB is somewhat
